{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abir Khan","familyName":"Ratul","name":"Ratul, Abir Khan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay","familyName":"Acharjee","name":"Acharjee, Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somin","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Somin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Nazmus","familyName":"Sakib","name":"Sakib, Md Nazmus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sketch2BIM: A Multi-Agent Human-AI Collaborative Pipeline to Convert Hand-Drawn Floor Plans to 3D BIM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-16T18:45:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-30T01:33:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.autcon.2026.106984","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study introduces a human-in-the-loop pipeline that converts unscaled, hand-drawn floor plan sketches into semantically consistent 3D BIM models. The workflow leverages multimodal large language models (MLLMs) within a multi-agent framework, combining perceptual extraction, human feedback, schema validation, and automated BIM scripting. Initially, sketches are iteratively refined into a structured JSON layout of walls, doors, and windows. Later, these layouts are transformed into executable scripts that generate 3D BIM models. Experiments on ten diverse floor plans demonstrate strong convergence: openings (doors, windows) are captured with high reliability in the initial pass, while wall detection begins around 83% and achieves near-perfect alignment after a few feedback iterations. Across all categories, precision, recall, and F1 scores remain above 0.83, and geometric errors (RMSE, MAE) progressively decrease to zero through feedback corrections. This study demonstrates how MLLM-driven multi-agent reasoning can make BIM creation accessible to both experts and non-experts using only freehand sketches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:40:36Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:40:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-30T04:01:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujin","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yujin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taesup","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taesup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Memory-Free Continual Learning with Null Space Adaptation for Zero-Shot Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T05:53:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:26:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T03:41:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:33:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained vision-language models (VLMs), such as CLIP, have demonstrated remarkable zero-shot generalization, enabling deployment in a wide range of real-world tasks without additional task-specific training. However, in real deployment scenarios with evolving environments or emerging classes, these models inevitably face distributional shifts and novel tasks. In such contexts, static zero-shot capabilities are insufficient, and there is a growing need for continual learning methods that allow models to adapt over time while avoiding catastrophic forgetting. We introduce NuSA-CL (Null Space Adaptation for Continual Learning), a lightweight memory-free continual learning framework designed to address this challenge. NuSA-CL employs low-rank adaptation and constrains task-specific weight updates to lie within an approximate null space of the model's current parameters. This strategy minimizes interference with previously acquired knowledge, effectively preserving the zero-shot capabilities of the original model. Unlike methods relying on replay buffers or costly distillation, NuSA-CL imposes minimal computational and memory overhead, making it practical for deployment in resource-constrained, real-world continual learning environments. Experiments show that our framework not only effectively preserves zero-shot transfer capabilities but also achieves highly competitive performance on continual learning benchmarks. These results position NuSA-CL as a practical and scalable solution for continually evolving zero-shot VLMs in real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at ICLR 2026. Code: https://github.com/ZoYujin/NuSA-CL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:30Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:43:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orest","familyName":"Kupyn","name":"Kupyn, Orest","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hirokatsu","familyName":"Kataoka","name":"Kataoka, Hirokatsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Rupprecht","name":"Rupprecht, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"S3OD: Towards Generalizable Salient Object Detection with Synthetic Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:10:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T17:08:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:24:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-17T16:14:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-18T01:05:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Salient object detection exemplifies data-bounded tasks where expensive pixel-precise annotations force separate model training for related subtasks like DIS and HR-SOD. We present a method that dramatically improves generalization through large-scale synthetic data generation and ambiguity-aware architecture. We introduce S3OD, a dataset of over 139,000 high-resolution images created through our multi-modal diffusion pipeline that extracts labels from diffusion and DINO-v3 features. The iterative generation framework prioritizes challenging categories based on model performance. We propose a streamlined multi-mask decoder that handles the inherent ambiguity in salient object detection by predicting multiple valid interpretations. Models trained only on synthetic data achieve 20-50% error reduction in cross-dataset generalization, while fine-tuned versions reach state-of-the-art performance across DIS and HR-SOD benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:31Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:19:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21122","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21122","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21122","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longtian","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Longtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shan","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Shan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NoisyGRPO: Incentivizing Multimodal CoT Reasoning via Noise Injection and Bayesian Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:23:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T07:06:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:32:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T14:13:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:26:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has shown promise in enhancing the general Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning capabilities of multimodal large language models (MLLMs). However, when applied to improve general CoT reasoning, existing RL frameworks often struggle to generalize beyond the training distribution. To address this, we propose NoisyGRPO, a systematic multimodal RL framework that introduces controllable noise into visual inputs for enhanced exploration and explicitly models the advantage estimation process via a Bayesian framework. Specifically, NoisyGRPO improves RL training by: (1) Noise-Injected Exploration Policy: Perturbing visual inputs with Gaussian noise to encourage exploration across a wider range of visual scenarios; and (2) Bayesian Advantage Estimation: Formulating advantage estimation as a principled Bayesian inference problem, where the injected noise level serves as a prior and the observed trajectory reward as the likelihood. This Bayesian modeling fuses both sources of information to compute a robust posterior estimate of trajectory advantage, effectively guiding MLLMs to prefer visually grounded trajectories over noisy ones. Experiments on standard CoT quality, general capability, and hallucination benchmarks demonstrate that NoisyGRPO substantially improves generalization and robustness, especially in RL settings with small-scale MLLMs such as Qwen2.5-VL 3B. The project page is available at https://artanic30.github.io/project_pages/NoisyGRPO/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Neurips 2025, Project page is available at https://artanic30.github.io/project_pages/NoisyGRPO/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21122","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:15Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:01:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minqi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Minqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhou","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jiazhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Sanner","name":"Sanner, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VOGUE: A Multimodal Dataset for Conversational Recommendation in Fashion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.5.2; H.3.3; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:45:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:24:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T16:26:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T01:20:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal conversational recommendation has recently emerged as a promising paradigm for delivering personalized experiences through natural dialogue enriched by visual and contextual grounding. Yet currently available multimodal conversational recommendation datasets remain limited: existing resources either simulate conversations, omit user history or fail to collect sufficiently detailed feedback, which constrain the types of research and evaluation they support.\n To address these gaps we introduce VOGUE, a dataset of 60 human human dialogues containing 2100 granularly labeled utterances in realistic fashion shopping scenarios. Each dialogue is paired with a shared visual catalogue, item metadata, user fashion profiles and post conversation ratings from both users (Seekers) and recommenders (Assistants). This design enables rigorous evaluation of conversational inference, including not only alignment between predicted and ground truth preferences but also calibration against full rating distributions and comparison with explicit and implicit user satisfaction signals.\n Our analyses of VOGUE reveal distinctive dynamics of visually grounded dialogue, e.g. recommenders frequently recommend items simultaneously in feature based groups, which creates distinct conversational phases bridged by Seeker critiques and refinements. Benchmarking Multimodal Large Language Models against human Recommenders shows that while MLLMs approach human level alignment in aggregate they exhibit systematic distribution errors in reproducing human ratings and struggle to generalize preference inference beyond explicitly discussed items. These findings establish VOGUE as both a unique resource for studying multimodal conversational systems and a challenge dataset beyond the current recommendation capabilities of existing top tier multimodal foundation models such as GPT-5-mini and Gemini-2.5-Flash."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:57Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21464","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21464","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21464","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjia","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Wenjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rushi","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Rushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dianbo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dianbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CXR-LanIC: Language-Grounded Interpretable Classifier for Chest X-Ray Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:46:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:51:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-22T13:47:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:53:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T04:25:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-18T00:25:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T08:29:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:44:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep learning models have achieved remarkable accuracy in chest X-ray diagnosis, yet their widespread clinical adoption remains limited by the black-box nature of their predictions. Clinicians require transparent, verifiable explanations to trust automated diagnoses and identify potential failure modes. We introduce CXR-LanIC (Language-Grounded Interpretable Classifier for Chest X-rays), a novel framework that addresses this interpretability challenge through task-aligned pattern discovery. Our approach trains transcoder-based sparse autoencoders on a BiomedCLIP diagnostic classifier to decompose medical image representations into interpretable visual patterns. By training an ensemble of 100 transcoders on multimodal embeddings from the MIMIC-CXR dataset, we discover approximately 5,000 monosemantic patterns spanning cardiac, pulmonary, pleural, structural, device, and artifact categories. Each pattern exhibits consistent activation behavior across images sharing specific radiological features, enabling transparent attribution where predictions decompose into 20-50 interpretable patterns with verifiable activation galleries. CXR-LanIC achieves competitive diagnostic accuracy on five key findings while providing the foundation for natural language explanations through planned large multimodal model annotation. Our key innovation lies in extracting interpretable features from a classifier trained on specific diagnostic objectives rather than general-purpose embeddings, ensuring discovered patterns are directly relevant to clinical decision-making, demonstrating that medical AI systems can be both accurate and interpretable, supporting safer clinical deployment through transparent, clinically grounded explanations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21464","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:20Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:46:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Rouhi","name":"Rouhi, Lucas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christophe","familyName":"Droz","name":"Droz, Christophe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tailoring dispersion and evanescent modes in multimodal nonlocal lattices using positive-only interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:01:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T13:56:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:58:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Metamaterials derive their unconventional properties from engineered microstructures, with periodic lattices providing a versatile framework for modeling wave propagation. Dispersion relations, obtained from Bloch-Floquet theory, govern how waves propagate, attenuate, or localize within such systems. Extending interactions beyond nearest neighbors, through nonlocality, substantially enriches the design space of band diagrams, enabling phenomena such as negative or zero group velocities, roton-like extrema, and band-gap localization. However, existing approaches to dispersion tailoring often rely on analytical formulations or Fourier-based identifications, which become impractical for complex coupling mechanisms and offer limited control over physical constraints such as stiffness positivity. This work introduces a general interpolation-based framework for customizing dispersion relations in uniform nonlocal lattices. Rather than reconstructing full dispersion curves, the method enforces prescribed frequency-wavenumber points as interpolation constraints, enabling localized and tunable control of wave behavior. The formulation is applied to both spring- and beam-interaction lattices, and demonstrated on an Euler-Bernoulli beam model with adjustable nonlocal couplings. Through systematic parameter tuning, the framework enables the creation of rotons, the adjustment of group-velocity dispersion, and the design of evanescent waves with controlled exponential decay within band gaps, all while ensuring real, positive-only stiffness parameters and passive mechanical behavior. Altogether, this parametric interpolation strategy provides a physically consistent and computationally efficient route for engineering advanced phononic functionalities in periodic nonlocal systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint also available on HAL: https://hal.science/hal-05330430"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:17:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21686","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21686","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21686","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raheem Karim","familyName":"Hashmani","name":"Hashmani, Raheem Karim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Garrett W.","familyName":"Merz","name":"Merz, Garrett W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helen","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Helen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariel","familyName":"Pettee","name":"Pettee, Mariel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Cranmer","name":"Cranmer, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multimodal Datasets with Controllable Mutual Information"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:04:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T07:46:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce a framework for generating highly multimodal datasets with explicitly calculable mutual information (MI) between modalities. This enables the construction of benchmark datasets that provide a novel testbed for systematic studies of mutual information estimators and multimodal self-supervised learning (SSL) techniques. Our framework constructs realistic datasets with known MI using a flow-based generative model and a structured causal framework for generating correlated latent variables. We benchmark a suite of MI estimators on datasets with varying ground truth MI values and verify that regression performance improves as the MI increases between input modalities and the target value. Finally, we describe how our framework can be applied to contexts including multi-detector astrophysics and SSL studies in the highly multimodal regime."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 7 figures, 2 tables. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/RKHashmani/MmMi-Datasets. Datasets generated based on Figure 1 can be found at https://huggingface.co/datasets/RKHashmani/mmmi-dag1-2modalities-cifar10"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21686","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:25Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sukanya","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Sukanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souhaib Ben","familyName":"Taieb","name":"Taieb, Souhaib Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Evidence-Based Post-Hoc Adjustment Framework for Anomaly Detection Under Data Contamination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:45:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:38:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T19:43:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:04:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised anomaly detection (AD) methods typically assume clean training data, yet real-world datasets often contain undetected or mislabeled anomalies, leading to significant performance degradation. Existing solutions require access to the training pipelines, data or prior knowledge of the proportions of anomalies in the data, limiting their real-world applicability. To address this challenge, we propose EPHAD, a simple yet effective test-time adaptation framework that updates the outputs of AD models trained on contaminated datasets using evidence gathered at test time. Our approach integrates the prior knowledge captured by the AD model trained on contaminated datasets with evidence derived from multimodal foundation models like Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP), classical AD methods like the Local Outlier Factor or domain-specific knowledge. We illustrate the intuition behind EPHAD using a synthetic toy example and validate its effectiveness through comprehensive experiments across eight visual AD datasets, twenty-six tabular AD datasets, and a real-world industrial AD dataset. Additionally, we conduct an ablation study to analyse hyperparameter influence and robustness to varying contamination levels, demonstrating the versatility and robustness of EPHAD across diverse AD models and evidence pairs. To ensure reproducibility, our code is publicly available at https://github.com/sukanyapatra1997/EPHAD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in the Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:23Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:42:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junzhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huixuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KBE-DME: Dynamic Multimodal Evaluation via Knowledge Enhanced Benchmark Evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:13:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:27:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T07:52:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-22T01:28:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid progress of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) calls for more reliable evaluation protocols. Existing static benchmarks suffer from the potential risk of data contamination and saturation, leading to inflated or misleading performance evaluations. To address these issues, we first apply Graph formulation to represent a static or dynamic VQA sample. With the formulation, we propose Knowledge-enhanced Benchmark Evolution(KBE), a dynamic multimodal evaluation framework. KBE first analyzes the original static benchmark, then expands it by integrating multimodal knowledge, transforming the static benchmark into a controllable, dynamic evolving version. Crucially, KBE can both reconstruct questions by Re-selecting visual information in the original image and expand existing questions with external textual knowledge. It enables difficulty-controllable evaluation by adjusting the degree of question exploration. Extensive experiments demonstrate that KBE alleviates the risk of data contamination, data saturation, and provides a more comprehensive assessment of MLLM capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:40Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-22T03:18:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Basile","name":"Basile, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentino","familyName":"Maiorca","name":"Maiorca, Valentino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego","familyName":"Doimo","name":"Doimo, Diego","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Locatello","name":"Locatello, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Cazzaniga","name":"Cazzaniga, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Head Pursuit: Probing Attention Specialization in Multimodal Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:41:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:54:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T15:47:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language and vision-language models have shown impressive performance across a wide range of tasks, but their internal mechanisms remain only partly understood. In this work, we study how individual attention heads in text-generative models specialize in specific semantic or visual attributes. Building on an established interpretability method, we reinterpret the practice of probing intermediate activations with the final decoding layer through the lens of signal processing. This lets us analyze multiple samples in a principled way and rank attention heads based on their relevance to target concepts. Our results show consistent patterns of specialization at the head level across both unimodal and multimodal transformers. Remarkably, we find that editing as few as 1% of the heads, selected using our method, can reliably suppress or enhance targeted concepts in the model output. We validate our approach on language tasks such as question answering and toxicity mitigation, as well as vision-language tasks including image classification and captioning. Our findings highlight an interpretable and controllable structure within attention layers, offering simple tools for understanding and editing large-scale generative models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 (spotlight)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:11:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrazek","familyName":"Abid","name":"Abid, Abderrazek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh-Cong","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Thanh-Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fakhri","familyName":"Karray","name":"Karray, Fakhri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vision Language Models for Dynamic Human Activity Recognition in Healthcare Settings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:04:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:48:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-08452-1_4","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As generative AI continues to evolve, Vision Language Models (VLMs) have emerged as promising tools in various healthcare applications. One area that remains relatively underexplored is their use in human activity recognition (HAR) for remote health monitoring. VLMs offer notable strengths, including greater flexibility and the ability to overcome some of the constraints of traditional deep learning models. However, a key challenge in applying VLMs to HAR lies in the difficulty of evaluating their dynamic and often non-deterministic outputs. To address this gap, we introduce a descriptive caption data set and propose comprehensive evaluation methods to evaluate VLMs in HAR. Through comparative experiments with state-of-the-art deep learning models, our findings demonstrate that VLMs achieve comparable performance and, in some cases, even surpass conventional approaches in terms of accuracy. This work contributes a strong benchmark and opens new possibilities for the integration of VLMs into intelligent healthcare systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:23Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:17:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21412","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21412","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21412","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Whie","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Whie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Semin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Semin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junee","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghoon","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Seunghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging the gap to real-world language-grounded visual concept learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T05:32:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:19:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human intelligence effortlessly interprets visual scenes along a rich spectrum of semantic dimensions. However, existing approaches to language-grounded visual concept learning are limited to a few predefined primitive axes, such as color and shape, and are typically explored in synthetic datasets. In this work, we propose a scalable framework that adaptively identifies image-related concept axes and grounds visual concepts along these axes in real-world scenes. Leveraging a pretrained vision-language model and our universal prompting strategy, our framework identifies a diverse image-related axes without any prior knowledge. Our universal concept encoder adaptively binds visual features to the discovered axes without introducing additional model parameters for each concept. To ground visual concepts along the discovered axes, we optimize a compositional anchoring objective, which ensures that each axis can be independently manipulated without affecting others. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our framework on subsets of ImageNet, CelebA-HQ, and AFHQ, showcasing superior editing capabilities across diverse real-world concepts that are too varied to be manually predefined. Our method also exhibits strong compositional generalization, outperforming existing visual concept learning and text-based editing methods. The code is available at https://github.com/whieya/Language-grounded-VCL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21412","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:07Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-12T03:31:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21451","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21451","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21451","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinglong","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Yinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunrong","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Chunrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalpel: Automotive Deep Learning Framework Testing via Assembling Model Components"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T14:18:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:50:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep learning (DL) plays a key role in autonomous driving systems. DL models support perception modules, equipped with tasks such as object detection and sensor fusion. These DL models enable vehicles to process multi-sensor inputs to understand complex surroundings. Deploying DL models in autonomous driving systems faces stringent challenges, including real-time processing, limited computational resources, and strict power constraints. To address these challenges, automotive DL frameworks (e.g., PaddleInference) have emerged to optimize inference efficiency. However, these frameworks encounter unique quality issues due to their more complex deployment environments, such as crashes stemming from limited scheduled memory and incorrect memory allocation. Unfortunately, existing DL framework testing methods fail to detect these quality issues due to the failure in deploying generated test input models, as these models lack three essential capabilities: (1) multi-input/output tensor processing, (2) multi-modal data processing, and (3) multi-level data feature extraction. These capabilities necessitate specialized model components, which existing testing methods neglect during model generation. To bridge this gap, we propose Scalpel, an automotive DL frameworks testing method that generates test input models at the model component level. Scalpel generates models by assembling model components (heads, necks, backbones) to support capabilities required by autonomous driving systems. Specifically, Scalpel maintains and updates a repository of model components, generating test inputs by selecting, mutating, and assembling them. Successfully generated models are added back to enrich the repository. Newly generated models are then deployed within the autonomous driving system to test automotive DL frameworks via differential testing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by the 48th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Software Engineering (ICSE 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21451","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:02Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-31T02:11:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21566","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21566","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21566","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangwen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fangwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunku","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yunku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiguang","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Ruiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Heyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huarong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Huarong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihui","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zhihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuosheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ColorEcosystem: Powering Personalized, Standardized, and Trustworthy Agentic Service in massive-agent Ecosystem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:26:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:57:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T07:12:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:26:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid development of (multimodal) large language model-based agents, the landscape of agentic service management has evolved from single-agent systems to multi-agent systems, and now to massive-agent ecosystems. Current massive-agent ecosystems face growing challenges, including impersonal service experiences, a lack of standardization, and untrustworthy behavior. To address these issues, we propose ColorEcosystem, a novel blueprint designed to enable personalized, standardized, and trustworthy agentic service at scale. Concretely, ColorEcosystem consists of three key components: agent carrier, agent store, and agent audit. The agent carrier provides personalized service experiences by utilizing user-specific data and creating a digital twin, while the agent store serves as a centralized, standardized platform for managing diverse agentic services. The agent audit, based on the supervision of developer and user activities, ensures the integrity and credibility of both service providers and users. Through the analysis of challenges, transitional forms, and practical considerations, the ColorEcosystem is poised to power personalized, standardized, and trustworthy agentic service across massive-agent ecosystems. Meanwhile, we have also implemented part of ColorEcosystem's functionality, and the relevant code is open-sourced at https://github.com/opas-lab/color-ecosystem."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21566","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sikuang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sikuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiemin","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Jiemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taoran","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Taoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhong","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Jiazhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingxi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Lingxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WorldGrow: Generating Infinite 3D World"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:39:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:04:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We tackle the challenge of generating the infinitely extendable 3D world -- large, continuous environments with coherent geometry and realistic appearance. Existing methods face key challenges: 2D-lifting approaches suffer from geometric and appearance inconsistencies across views, 3D implicit representations are hard to scale up, and current 3D foundation models are mostly object-centric, limiting their applicability to scene-level generation. Our key insight is leveraging strong generation priors from pre-trained 3D models for structured scene block generation. To this end, we propose WorldGrow, a hierarchical framework for unbounded 3D scene synthesis. Our method features three core components: (1) a data curation pipeline that extracts high-quality scene blocks for training, making the 3D structured latent representations suitable for scene generation; (2) a 3D block inpainting mechanism that enables context-aware scene extension; and (3) a coarse-to-fine generation strategy that ensures both global layout plausibility and local geometric/textural fidelity. Evaluated on the large-scale 3D-FRONT dataset, WorldGrow achieves SOTA performance in geometry reconstruction, while uniquely supporting infinite scene generation with photorealistic and structurally consistent outputs. These results highlight its capability for constructing large-scale virtual environments and potential for building future world models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://world-grow.github.io/ Code: https://github.com/world-grow/WorldGrow"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:19Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21679","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21679","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21679","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaku","familyName":"Morio","name":"Morio, Gaku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harri","familyName":"Rowlands","name":"Rowlands, Harri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominik","familyName":"Stammbach","name":"Stammbach, Dominik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher D.","familyName":"Manning","name":"Manning, Christopher D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multimodal Benchmark for Framing of Oil \u0026amp; Gas Advertising and Potential Greenwashing Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:34:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:03:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Companies spend large amounts of money on public relations campaigns to project a positive brand image. However, sometimes there is a mismatch between what they say and what they do. Oil \u0026amp; gas companies, for example, are accused of \"greenwashing\" with imagery of climate-friendly initiatives. Understanding the framing, and changes in framing, at scale can help better understand the goals and nature of public relations campaigns. To address this, we introduce a benchmark dataset of expert-annotated video ads obtained from Facebook and YouTube. The dataset provides annotations for 13 framing types for more than 50 companies or advocacy groups across 20 countries. Our dataset is especially designed for the evaluation of vision-language models (VLMs), distinguishing it from past text-only framing datasets. Baseline experiments show some promising results, while leaving room for improvement for future work: GPT-4.1 can detect environmental messages with 79% F1 score, while our best model only achieves 46% F1 score on identifying framing around green innovation. We also identify challenges that VLMs must address, such as implicit framing, handling videos of various lengths, or implicit cultural backgrounds. Our dataset contributes to research in multimodal analysis of strategic communication in the energy sector."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Forthcoming in NeurIPS 2025 Datasets and Benchmarks Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21679","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:14Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joey Tianyi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Joey Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuozhu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zuozhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modest-Align: Data-Efficient Alignment for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:11:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-modal alignment aims to map heterogeneous modalities into a shared latent space, as exemplified by models like CLIP, which benefit from large-scale image-text pretraining for strong recognition capabilities. However, when operating in resource-constrained settings with limited or low-quality data, these models often suffer from overconfidence and degraded performance due to the prevalence of ambiguous or weakly correlated image-text pairs. Current contrastive learning approaches, which rely on single positive pairs, further exacerbate this issue by reinforcing overconfidence on uncertain samples. To address these challenges, we propose Modest-Align, a lightweight alignment framework designed for robustness and efficiency. Our approach leverages two complementary strategies -- Random Perturbation, which introduces controlled noise to simulate uncertainty, and Embedding Smoothing, which calibrates similarity distributions in the embedding space. These mechanisms collectively reduce overconfidence and improve performance on noisy or weakly aligned samples. Extensive experiments across multiple benchmark datasets demonstrate that Modest-Align outperforms state-of-the-art methods in retrieval tasks, achieving competitive results with over 100x less training data and 600x less GPU time than CLIP. Our method offers a practical and scalable solution for cross-modal alignment in real-world, low-resource scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuicai","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Kuicai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shurui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangda","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Fangda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dexun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dexun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Doc-Researcher: A Unified System for Multimodal Document Parsing and Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:07:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep Research systems have revolutionized how LLMs solve complex questions through iterative reasoning and evidence gathering. However, current systems remain fundamentally constrained to textual web data, overlooking the vast knowledge embedded in multimodal documents Processing such documents demands sophisticated parsing to preserve visual semantics (figures, tables, charts, and equations), intelligent chunking to maintain structural coherence, and adaptive retrieval across modalities, which are capabilities absent in existing systems. In response, we present Doc-Researcher, a unified system that bridges this gap through three integrated components: (i) deep multimodal parsing that preserves layout structure and visual semantics while creating multi-granular representations from chunk to document level, (ii) systematic retrieval architecture supporting text-only, vision-only, and hybrid paradigms with dynamic granularity selection, and (iii) iterative multi-agent workflows that decompose complex queries, progressively accumulate evidence, and synthesize comprehensive answers across documents and modalities. To enable rigorous evaluation, we introduce M4DocBench, the first benchmark for Multi-modal, Multi-hop, Multi-document, and Multi-turn deep research. Featuring 158 expert-annotated questions with complete evidence chains across 304 documents, M4DocBench tests capabilities that existing benchmarks cannot assess. Experiments demonstrate that Doc-Researcher achieves 50.6% accuracy, 3.4xbetter than state-of-the-art baselines, validating that effective document research requires not just better retrieval, but fundamentally deep parsing that preserve multimodal integrity and support iterative research. Our work establishes a new paradigm for conducting deep research on multimodal document collections."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:29Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ciara","familyName":"Rowles","name":"Rowles, Ciara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Jampani","name":"Jampani, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Donné","name":"Donné, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimon","familyName":"Vainer","name":"Vainer, Shimon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Parker","name":"Parker, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zach","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, Zach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Foley Control: Aligning a Frozen Latent Text-to-Audio Model to Video"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:49:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foley Control is a lightweight approach to video-guided Foley that keeps pretrained single-modality models frozen and learns only a small cross-attention bridge between them. We connect V-JEPA2 video embeddings to a frozen Stable Audio Open DiT text-to-audio (T2A) model by inserting compact video cross-attention after the model's existing text cross-attention, so prompts set global semantics while video refines timing and local dynamics. The frozen backbones retain strong marginals (video; audio given text) and the bridge learns the audio-video dependency needed for synchronization -- without retraining the audio prior. To cut memory and stabilize training, we pool video tokens before conditioning. On curated video-audio benchmarks, Foley Control delivers competitive temporal and semantic alignment with far fewer trainable parameters than recent multi-modal systems, while preserving prompt-driven controllability and production-friendly modularity (swap/upgrade encoders or the T2A backbone without end-to-end retraining). Although we focus on Video-to-Foley, the same bridge design can potentially extend to other audio modalities (e.g., speech)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://stability-ai.github.io/foleycontrol.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qixiu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qixiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaobo","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengtang","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chengtang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingqi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huizhi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Huizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lily","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaolong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baining","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Baining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Vision-Language-Action Model Pretraining for Robotic Manipulation with Real-Life Human Activity Videos"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:39:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a novel approach for pretraining robotic manipulation Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models using a large corpus of unscripted real-life video recordings of human hand activities. Treating human hand as dexterous robot end-effector, we show that \"in-the-wild\" egocentric human videos without any annotations can be transformed into data formats fully aligned with existing robotic V-L-A training data in terms of task granularity and labels. This is achieved by the development of a fully-automated holistic human activity analysis approach for arbitrary human hand videos. This approach can generate atomic-level hand activity segments and their language descriptions, each accompanied with framewise 3D hand motion and camera motion. We process a large volume of egocentric videos and create a hand-VLA training dataset containing 1M episodes and 26M frames. This training data covers a wide range of objects and concepts, dexterous manipulation tasks, and environment variations in real life, vastly exceeding the coverage of existing robot data. We design a dexterous hand VLA model architecture and pretrain the model on this dataset. The model exhibits strong zero-shot capabilities on completely unseen real-world observations. Additionally, fine-tuning it on a small amount of real robot action data significantly improves task success rates and generalization to novel objects in real robotic experiments. We also demonstrate the appealing scaling behavior of the model's task performance with respect to pretraining data scale. We believe this work lays a solid foundation for scalable VLA pretraining, advancing robots toward truly generalizable embodied intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://microsoft.github.io/VITRA/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:45Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21553","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21553","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21553","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Claypoole","name":"Claypoole, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunye","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Yunye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noson S.","familyName":"Yanofsky","name":"Yanofsky, Noson S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajay","familyName":"Divakaran","name":"Divakaran, Ajay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Document Understanding, Measurement, and Manipulation Using Category Theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:12:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:57:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We apply category theory to extract multimodal document structure which leads us to develop information theoretic measures, content summarization and extension, and self-supervised improvement of large pretrained models. We first develop a mathematical representation of a document as a category of question-answer pairs. Second, we develop an orthogonalization procedure to divide the information contained in one or more documents into non-overlapping pieces. The structures extracted in the first and second steps lead us to develop methods to measure and enumerate the information contained in a document. We also build on those steps to develop new summarization techniques, as well as to develop a solution to a new problem viz. exegesis resulting in an extension of the original document. Our question-answer pair methodology enables a novel rate distortion analysis of summarization techniques. We implement our techniques using large pretrained models, and we propose a multimodal extension of our overall mathematical framework. Finally, we develop a novel self-supervised method using RLVR to improve large pretrained models using consistency constraints such as composability and closure under certain operations that stem naturally from our category theoretic framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21553","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:20Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21551","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21551","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21551","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jialu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung Manh","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Hung Manh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignace","familyName":"De Lathauwer","name":"De Lathauwer, Ignace","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henk S.","familyName":"Schipper","name":"Schipper, Henk S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaqib","familyName":"Saeed","name":"Saeed, Aaqib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interpretable Multimodal Zero-Shot ECG Diagnosis via Structured Clinical Knowledge Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:09:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:56:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electrocardiogram (ECG) interpretation is essential for cardiovascular disease diagnosis, but current automated systems often struggle with transparency and generalization to unseen conditions. To address this, we introduce ZETA, a zero-shot multimodal framework designed for interpretable ECG diagnosis aligned with clinical workflows. ZETA uniquely compares ECG signals against structured positive and negative clinical observations, which are curated through an LLM-assisted, expert-validated process, thereby mimicking differential diagnosis. Our approach leverages a pre-trained multimodal model to align ECG and text embeddings without disease-specific fine-tuning. Empirical evaluations demonstrate ZETA's competitive zero-shot classification performance and, importantly, provide qualitative and quantitative evidence of enhanced interpretability, grounding predictions in specific, clinically relevant positive and negative diagnostic features. ZETA underscores the potential of aligning ECG analysis with structured clinical knowledge for building more transparent, generalizable, and trustworthy AI diagnostic systems. We will release the curated observation dataset and code to facilitate future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21551","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21501","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21501","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21501","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Guanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ruoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peisen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Peisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenrui","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Wenrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junni","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Junni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkai","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Hongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaopeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GranViT: A Fine-Grained Vision Model With Autoregressive Perception For MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T12:33:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision encoders are indispensable for allowing impressive performance of Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) in vision language tasks such as visual question answering and reasoning. However, existing vision encoders focus on global image representations but overlook fine-grained regional analysis. They are limited in fine grained perception due to the scarcity of fine grained annotated data and the lack of a fine grained pre-training paradigm. In this paper, we propose GranViT, a novel Vision Transformer that integrates fine-grained feature extraction with semantic alignment to Large Language Models (LLMs) via region level autoregressive training. We first construct Gran-29M, a dataset comprising 2million natural and OCR images paired with over 180 million high-quality region-level annotations, to enable large scale fine grained pretraining. Consequently, we develop a pretraining-adaptation framework along with a self distillation mechanism to train fine-grained GranViT on Gran-29M. We sufficiently exploit the fine-grained annotations from Gran-29M to resort to bounding-box-to-caption regression to enhance localized visual representation of the vision encoder in the pretraining and caption-to-bounding-box regression to improve vision feature utilization and localization for LLM in the adaptation. We further incorporate a self distillation mechanism that imposes explicit localization constraints on the vision encoder to strengthen its regional reasoning capability. Extensive experiments show that GranViT surpasses existing vision encoders and attains strong transferability to varying LLMs. Remarkably, it achieves state-of-the-art results on fine-grained recognition, multimodal VQA, and OCR understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21501","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengtian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shengtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingshi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yingshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingrou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingrou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoniTor: Exploiting Large Language Models with Instruction for Online Video Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:28:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Anomaly Detection (VAD) aims to locate unusual activities or behaviors within videos. Recently, offline VAD has garnered substantial research attention, which has been invigorated by the progress in large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs), offering the potential for a more nuanced understanding of anomalies. However, online VAD has seldom received attention due to real-time constraints and computational intensity. In this paper, we introduce a novel Memory-based online scoring queue scheme for Training-free VAD (MoniTor), to address the inherent complexities in online VAD. Specifically, MoniTor applies a streaming input to VLMs, leveraging the capabilities of pre-trained large-scale models. To capture temporal dependencies more effectively, we incorporate a novel prediction mechanism inspired by Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks. This ensures the model can effectively model past states and leverage previous predictions to identify anomalous behaviors. Thereby, it better understands the current frame. Moreover, we design a scoring queue and an anomaly prior to dynamically store recent scores and cover all anomalies in the monitoring scenario, providing guidance for LLMs to distinguish between normal and abnormal behaviors over time. We evaluate MoniTor on two large datasets (i.e., UCF-Crime and XD-Violence) containing various surveillance and real-world scenarios. The results demonstrate that MoniTor outperforms state-of-the-art methods and is competitive with weakly supervised methods without training. Code is available at https://github.com/YsTvT/MoniTor."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025. The first two authors hold equal contributions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21447","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21447","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21447","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhilu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhilu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wangmeng","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Wangmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PhysWorld: From Real Videos to World Models of Deformable Objects via Physics-Aware Demonstration Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:25:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interactive world models that simulate object dynamics are crucial for robotics, VR, and AR. However, it remains a significant challenge to learn physics-consistent dynamics models from limited real-world video data, especially for deformable objects with spatially-varying physical properties. To overcome the challenge of data scarcity, we propose PhysWorld, a novel framework that utilizes a simulator to synthesize physically plausible and diverse demonstrations to learn efficient world models. Specifically, we first construct a physics-consistent digital twin within MPM simulator via constitutive model selection and global-to-local optimization of physical properties. Subsequently, we apply part-aware perturbations to the physical properties and generate various motion patterns for the digital twin, synthesizing extensive and diverse demonstrations. Finally, using these demonstrations, we train a lightweight GNN-based world model that is embedded with physical properties. The real video can be used to further refine the physical properties. PhysWorld achieves accurate and fast future predictions for various deformable objects, and also generalizes well to novel interactions. Experiments show that PhysWorld has competitive performance while enabling inference speeds 47 times faster than the recent state-of-the-art method, i.e., PhysTwin."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21447","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:56Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh Cong","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Thanh Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farah","familyName":"Kharrat","name":"Kharrat, Farah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrazek","familyName":"Abid","name":"Abid, Abderrazek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fakhri","familyName":"Karray","name":"Karray, Fakhri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REMONI: An Autonomous System Integrating Wearables and Multimodal Large Language Models for Enhanced Remote Health Monitoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:23:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/memea60663.2024.10596778","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the widespread adoption of wearable devices in our daily lives, the demand and appeal for remote patient monitoring have significantly increased. Most research in this field has concentrated on collecting sensor data, visualizing it, and analyzing it to detect anomalies in specific diseases such as diabetes, heart disease and depression. However, this domain has a notable gap in the aspect of human-machine interaction. This paper proposes REMONI, an autonomous REmote health MONItoring system that integrates multimodal large language models (MLLMs), the Internet of Things (IoT), and wearable devices. The system automatically and continuously collects vital signs, accelerometer data from a special wearable (such as a smartwatch), and visual data in patient video clips collected from cameras. This data is processed by an anomaly detection module, which includes a fall detection model and algorithms to identify and alert caregivers of the patient's emergency conditions. A distinctive feature of our proposed system is the natural language processing component, developed with MLLMs capable of detecting and recognizing a patient's activity and emotion while responding to healthcare worker's inquiries. Additionally, prompt engineering is employed to integrate all patient information seamlessly. As a result, doctors and nurses can access real-time vital signs and the patient's current state and mood by interacting with an intelligent agent through a user-friendly web application. Our experiments demonstrate that our system is implementable and scalable for real-life scenarios, potentially reducing the workload of medical professionals and healthcare costs. A full-fledged prototype illustrating the functionalities of the system has been developed and being tested to demonstrate the robustness of its various capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21438","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21438","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21438","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satheeshkumar","familyName":"Veeramani","name":"Veeramani, Satheeshkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengxue","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhengxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francisco","familyName":"Munguia-Galeano","name":"Munguia-Galeano, Francisco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hatem","familyName":"Fakhruldeen","name":"Fakhruldeen, Hatem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Roddelkopf","name":"Roddelkopf, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed Faeik Ruzaij","familyName":"Al-Okby","name":"Al-Okby, Mohammed Faeik Ruzaij","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kerstin","familyName":"Thurow","name":"Thurow, Kerstin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew Ian","familyName":"Cooper","name":"Cooper, Andrew Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PREVENT: Proactive Risk Evaluation and Vigilant Execution of Tasks for Mobile Robotic Chemists using Multi-Modal Behavior Trees"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:16:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:49:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mobile robotic chemists are a fast growing trend in the field of chemistry and materials research. However, so far these mobile robots lack workflow awareness skills. This poses the risk that even a small anomaly, such as an improperly capped sample vial could disrupt the entire workflow. This wastes time, and resources, and could pose risks to human researchers, such as exposure to toxic materials. Existing perception mechanisms can be used to predict anomalies but they often generate excessive false positives. This may halt workflow execution unnecessarily, requiring researchers to intervene and to resume the workflow when no problem actually exists, negating the benefits of autonomous operation. To address this problem, we propose PREVENT a system comprising navigation and manipulation skills based on a multimodal Behavior Tree (BT) approach that can be integrated into existing software architectures with minimal modifications. Our approach involves a hierarchical perception mechanism that exploits AI techniques and sensory feedback through Dexterous Vision and Navigational Vision cameras and an IoT gas sensor module for execution-related decision-making. Experimental evaluations show that the proposed approach is comparatively efficient and completely avoids both false negatives and false positives when tested in simulated risk scenarios within our robotic chemistry workflow. The results also show that the proposed multi-modal perception skills achieved deployment accuracies that were higher than the average of the corresponding uni-modal skills, both for navigation and for manipulation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 8 figures, paper submitted to Robotics and Autonomous Systems Journal"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21438","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:43Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21418","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21418","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21418","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Bierling","name":"Bierling, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Pasero","name":"Pasero, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan-Henrik","familyName":"Bertrand","name":"Bertrand, Jan-Henrik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiki","familyName":"Van Gerwen","name":"Van Gerwen, Kiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DreamerV3-XP: Optimizing exploration through uncertainty estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:58:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:48:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce DreamerV3-XP, an extension of DreamerV3 that improves exploration and learning efficiency. This includes (i) a prioritized replay buffer, scoring trajectories by return, reconstruction loss, and value error and (ii) an intrinsic reward based on disagreement over predicted environment rewards from an ensemble of world models. DreamerV3-XP is evaluated on a subset of Atari100k and DeepMind Control Visual Benchmark tasks, confirming the original DreamerV3 results and showing that our extensions lead to faster learning and lower dynamics model loss, particularly in sparse-reward settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21418","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:15Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21406","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21406","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21406","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwei","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jinwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qijia","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Qijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhen","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingzhi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiping","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Shiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan Q.","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ethan Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wentong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Limin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Limin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MUVR: A Multi-Modal Untrimmed Video Retrieval Benchmark with Multi-Level Visual Correspondence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:50:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose the Multi-modal Untrimmed Video Retrieval task, along with a new benchmark (MUVR) to advance video retrieval for long-video platforms. MUVR aims to retrieve untrimmed videos containing relevant segments using multi-modal queries. It has the following features: 1) Practical retrieval paradigm: MUVR supports video-centric multi-modal queries, expressing fine-grained retrieval needs through long text descriptions, video tag prompts, and mask prompts. It adopts a one-to-many retrieval paradigm and focuses on untrimmed videos, tailored for long-video platform applications. 2) Multi-level visual correspondence: To cover common video categories (e.g., news, travel, dance) and precisely define retrieval matching criteria, we construct multi-level visual correspondence based on core video content (e.g., news events, travel locations, dance moves) which users are interested in and want to retrieve. It covers six levels: copy, event, scene, instance, action, and others. 3) Comprehensive evaluation criteria: We develop 3 versions of MUVR (i.e., Base, Filter, QA). MUVR-Base/Filter evaluates retrieval models, while MUVR-QA assesses MLLMs in a question-answering format. We also propose a Reranking Score to evaluate the reranking ability of MLLMs. MUVR consists of 53K untrimmed videos from the video platform Bilibili, with 1,050 multi-modal queries and 84K matches. Extensive evaluations of 3 state-of-the-art video retrieval models, 6 image-based VLMs, and 10 MLLMs are conducted. MUVR reveals the limitations of retrieval methods in processing untrimmed videos and multi-modal queries, as well as MLLMs in multi-video understanding and reranking. Our code and benchmark is available at https://github.com/debby-0527/MUVR."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025 D\u0026amp;B Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21406","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:58Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:53:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21346","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21346","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21346","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lemin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangchao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Fangchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghua","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Honghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaru","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yaru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Limin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Limin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongliang","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Yongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CT-CLIP: A Multi-modal Fusion Framework for Robust Apple Leaf Disease Recognition in Complex Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:23:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:43:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In complex orchard environments, the phenotypic heterogeneity of different apple leaf diseases, characterized by significant variation among lesions, poses a challenge to traditional multi-scale feature fusion methods. These methods only integrate multi-layer features extracted by convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and fail to adequately account for the relationships between local and global features. Therefore, this study proposes a multi-branch recognition framework named CNN-Transformer-CLIP (CT-CLIP). The framework synergistically employs a CNN to extract local lesion detail features and a Vision Transformer to capture global structural relationships. An Adaptive Feature Fusion Module (AFFM) then dynamically fuses these features, achieving optimal coupling of local and global information and effectively addressing the diversity in lesion morphology and distribution. Additionally, to mitigate interference from complex backgrounds and significantly enhance recognition accuracy under few-shot conditions, this study proposes a multimodal image-text learning approach. By leveraging pre-trained CLIP weights, it achieves deep alignment between visual features and disease semantic descriptions. Experimental results show that CT-CLIP achieves accuracies of 97.38% and 96.12% on a publicly available apple disease and a self-built dataset, outperforming several baseline methods. The proposed CT-CLIP demonstrates strong capabilities in recognizing agricultural diseases, significantly enhances identification accuracy under complex environmental conditions, provides an innovative and practical solution for automated disease recognition in agricultural applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21346","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21323","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21323","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21323","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shufan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Shufan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junshu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Junshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VL-SAE: Interpreting and Enhancing Vision-Language Alignment with a Unified Concept Set"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:29:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:40:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The alignment of vision-language representations endows current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) with strong multi-modal reasoning capabilities. However, the interpretability of the alignment component remains uninvestigated due to the difficulty in mapping the semantics of multi-modal representations into a unified concept set. To address this problem, we propose VL-SAE, a sparse autoencoder that encodes vision-language representations into its hidden activations. Each neuron in its hidden layer correlates to a concept represented by semantically similar images and texts, thereby interpreting these representations with a unified concept set. To establish the neuron-concept correlation, we encourage semantically similar representations to exhibit consistent neuron activations during self-supervised training. First, to measure the semantic similarity of multi-modal representations, we perform their alignment in an explicit form based on cosine similarity. Second, we construct the VL-SAE with a distance-based encoder and two modality-specific decoders to ensure the activation consistency of semantically similar representations. Experiments across multiple VLMs (e.g., CLIP, LLaVA) demonstrate the superior capability of VL-SAE in interpreting and enhancing the vision-language alignment. For interpretation, the alignment between vision and language representations can be understood by comparing their semantics with concepts. For enhancement, the alignment can be strengthened by aligning vision-language representations at the concept level, contributing to performance improvements in downstream tasks, including zero-shot image classification and hallucination elimination. Codes are available at https://github.com/ssfgunner/VL-SAE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21323","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:02Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazuo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiazuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haomiao","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Haomiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhi","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Yunzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huchuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Huchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"He","name":"He, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FineRS: Fine-grained Reasoning and Segmentation of Small Objects with Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:14:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities across a wide range of vision-language tasks. However, due to the restricted input resolutions, MLLMs face significant challenges in precisely understanding and localizing visual details in high-resolution images -- particularly when dealing with extra-small objects embedded in cluttered contexts. To address this issue, we propose \\textsc{FineRS}, a two-stage MLLM-based reinforcement learning framework for jointly reasoning and segmenting extremely small objects within high-resolution scenes. \\textsc{FineRS} adopts a coarse-to-fine pipeline comprising Global Semantic Exploration (GSE) and Localized Perceptual Refinement (LPR). Specifically, GSE performs instruction-guided reasoning to generate a textural response and a coarse target region, while LPR refines this region to produce an accurate bounding box and segmentation mask. To couple the two stages, we introduce a locate-informed retrospective reward, where LPR's outputs are used to optimize GSE for more robust coarse region exploration. % Additionally, we present \\textsc{FineRS}-4k, a new dataset for evaluating MLLMs on attribute-level reasoning and pixel-level segmentation on subtle, small-scale targets in complex high-resolution scenes. Experimental results on \\textsc{FineRS}-4k and public datasets demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms state-of-the-art MLLM-based approaches on both instruction-guided segmentation and visual reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:44Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:51:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Scool, CRIStAL"],"givenName":"Waris","familyName":"Radji","name":"Radji, Waris","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Scool, CRIStAL"],"givenName":"Odalric-Ambrym","familyName":"Maillard","name":"Maillard, Odalric-Ambrym","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Hard is it to Confuse a World Model?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:08:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:32:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In reinforcement learning (RL) theory, the concept of most confusing instances is central to establishing regret lower bounds, that is, the minimal exploration needed to solve a problem. Given a reference model and its optimal policy, a most confusing instance is the statistically closest alternative model that makes a suboptimal policy optimal. While this concept is well-studied in multi-armed bandits and ergodic tabular Markov decision processes, constructing such instances remains an open question in the general case. In this paper, we formalize this problem for neural network world models as a constrained optimization: finding a modified model that is statistically close to the reference one, while producing divergent performance between optimal and suboptimal policies. We propose an adversarial training procedure to solve this problem and conduct an empirical study across world models of varying quality. Our results suggest that the degree of achievable confusion correlates with uncertainty in the approximate model, which may inform theoretically-grounded exploration strategies for deep model-based RL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengdong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chengdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Weidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song-Chun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Song-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaodong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"World Models Should Prioritize the Unification of Physical and Social Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:42:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:31:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"World models, which explicitly learn environmental dynamics to lay the foundation for planning, reasoning, and decision-making, are rapidly advancing in predicting both physical dynamics and aspects of social behavior, yet predominantly in separate silos. This division results in a systemic failure to model the crucial interplay between physical environments and social constructs, rendering current models fundamentally incapable of adequately addressing the true complexity of real-world systems where physical and social realities are inextricably intertwined. This position paper argues that the systematic, bidirectional unification of physical and social predictive capabilities is the next crucial frontier for world model development. We contend that comprehensive world models must holistically integrate objective physical laws with the subjective, evolving, and context-dependent nature of social dynamics. Such unification is paramount for AI to robustly navigate complex real-world challenges and achieve more generalizable intelligence. This paper substantiates this imperative by analyzing core impediments to integration, proposing foundational guiding principles (ACE Principles), and outlining a conceptual framework alongside a research roadmap towards truly holistic world models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwei","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Xingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kar Wai","familyName":"Fok","name":"Fok, Kar Wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vrizlynn L. L.","familyName":"Thing","name":"Thing, Vrizlynn L. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:35:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:31:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) comprise of both visual and textual modalities to process vision language tasks. However, MLLMs are vulnerable to security-related issues, such as jailbreak attacks that alter the model's input to induce unauthorized or harmful responses. The incorporation of the additional visual modality introduces new dimensions to security threats. In this paper, we proposed a black-box jailbreak method via both text and image prompts to evaluate MLLMs. In particular, we designed text prompts with provocative instructions, along with image prompts that introduced mutation and multi-image capabilities. To strengthen the evaluation, we also designed a Re-attack strategy. Empirical results show that our proposed work can improve capabilities to assess the security of both open-source and closed-source MLLMs. With that, we identified gaps in existing defense methods to propose new strategies for both training-time and inference-time defense methods, and evaluated them across the new jailbreak methods. The experiment results showed that the re-designed defense methods improved protections against the jailbreak attacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:26Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21204","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21204","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21204","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danielle C.","familyName":"Maddix","name":"Maddix, Danielle C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junming","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Junming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Erickson","name":"Erickson, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdul Fatir","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Abdul Fatir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boran","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Boran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leman","familyName":"Akoglu","name":"Akoglu, Leman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Faloutsos","name":"Faloutsos, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cuixiong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Cuixiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huzefa","familyName":"Rangwala","name":"Rangwala, Huzefa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Karypis","name":"Karypis, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bernie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:15:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:30:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Since the seminal work of TabPFN, research on tabular foundation models (TFMs) based on in-context learning (ICL) has challenged long-standing paradigms in machine learning. Without seeing any real-world data, models pretrained on purely synthetic datasets generalize remarkably well across diverse datasets, often using only a moderate number of in-context examples. This shifts the focus in tabular machine learning from model architecture design to the design of synthetic datasets, or, more precisely, to the prior distributions that generate them. Yet the guiding principles for prior design remain poorly understood. This work marks the first attempt to address the gap. We systematically investigate and identify key properties of synthetic priors that allow pretrained TFMs to generalize well. Based on these insights, we introduce Mitra, a TFM trained on a curated mixture of synthetic priors selected for their diversity, distinctiveness, and performance on real-world tabular data. Mitra consistently outperforms state-of-the-art TFMs, such as TabPFNv2 and TabICL, across both classification and regression benchmarks, with better sample efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. We released both classifier (autogluon/mitra-classifier) and regressor (autogluon/mitra-regressor) model weights on HuggingFace"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21204","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:12Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21160","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21160","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21160","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanlin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Guanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Boyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao Frank","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hao Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Physics-informed Spatial Intelligence with Human Priors: An Autonomous Driving Pilot Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T05:21:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:25:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"How to integrate and verify spatial intelligence in foundation models remains an open challenge. Current practice often proxies Visual-Spatial Intelligence (VSI) with purely textual prompts and VQA-style scoring, which obscures geometry, invites linguistic shortcuts, and weakens attribution to genuinely spatial skills. We introduce Spatial Intelligence Grid (SIG): a structured, grid-based schema that explicitly encodes object layouts, inter-object relations, and physically grounded priors. As a complementary channel to text, SIG provides a faithful, compositional representation of scene structure for foundation-model reasoning. Building on SIG, we derive SIG-informed evaluation metrics that quantify a model's intrinsic VSI, which separates spatial capability from language priors. In few-shot in-context learning with state-of-the-art multimodal LLMs (e.g. GPT- and Gemini-family models), SIG yields consistently larger, more stable, and more comprehensive gains across all VSI metrics compared to VQA-only representations, indicating its promise as a data-labeling and training schema for learning VSI. We also release SIGBench, a benchmark of 1.4K driving frames annotated with ground-truth SIG labels and human gaze traces, supporting both grid-based machine VSI tasks and attention-driven, human-like VSI tasks in autonomous-driving scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 (Spotlight)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21160","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:09Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21121","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21121","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21121","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boce","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Boce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xupeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Howell","name":"Howell, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Walters","name":"Walters, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Platt","name":"Platt, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalizable Hierarchical Skill Learning via Object-Centric Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:21:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Generalizable Hierarchical Skill Learning (GSL), a novel framework for hierarchical policy learning that significantly improves policy generalization and sample efficiency in robot manipulation. One core idea of GSL is to use object-centric skills as an interface that bridges the high-level vision-language model and the low-level visual-motor policy. Specifically, GSL decomposes demonstrations into transferable and object-canonicalized skill primitives using foundation models, ensuring efficient low-level skill learning in the object frame. At test time, the skill-object pairs predicted by the high-level agent are fed to the low-level module, where the inferred canonical actions are mapped back to the world frame for execution. This structured yet flexible design leads to substantial improvements in sample efficiency and generalization of our method across unseen spatial arrangements, object appearances, and task compositions. In simulation, GSL trained with only 3 demonstrations per task outperforms baselines trained with 30 times more data by 15.5 percent on unseen tasks. In real-world experiments, GSL also surpasses the baseline trained with 10 times more data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21121","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:14Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21120","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21120","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21120","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alec","familyName":"Helbling","name":"Helbling, Alec","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shruti","familyName":"Palaskar","name":"Palaskar, Shruti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kundan","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Kundan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Polo","familyName":"Chau","name":"Chau, Polo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Gatys","name":"Gatys, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph Yitan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Joseph Yitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SafetyPairs: Isolating Safety Critical Image Features with Counterfactual Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:19:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"What exactly makes a particular image unsafe? Systematically differentiating between benign and problematic images is a challenging problem, as subtle changes to an image, such as an insulting gesture or symbol, can drastically alter its safety implications. However, existing image safety datasets are coarse and ambiguous, offering only broad safety labels without isolating the specific features that drive these differences. We introduce SafetyPairs, a scalable framework for generating counterfactual pairs of images, that differ only in the features relevant to the given safety policy, thus flipping their safety label. By leveraging image editing models, we make targeted changes to images that alter their safety labels while leaving safety-irrelevant details unchanged. Using SafetyPairs, we construct a new safety benchmark, which serves as a powerful source of evaluation data that highlights weaknesses in vision-language models' abilities to distinguish between subtly different images. Beyond evaluation, we find our pipeline serves as an effective data augmentation strategy that improves the sample efficiency of training lightweight guard models. We release a benchmark containing over 3,020 SafetyPair images spanning a diverse taxonomy of 9 safety categories, providing the first systematic resource for studying fine-grained image safety distinctions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21120","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:12Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21111","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21111","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21111","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Weijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuantang","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xuantang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manli","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Manli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoyang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghui","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Honghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinqiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PhysVLM-AVR: Active Visual Reasoning for Multimodal Large Language Models in Physical Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:20:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual reasoning in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) has primarily been studied in static, fully observable settings, limiting their effectiveness in real-world environments where information is often incomplete due to occlusion or limited field of view. Humans, in contrast, actively explore and interact with their environment-moving, examining, and manipulating objects-to gather information through a closed-loop process integrating perception, reasoning, and action. Inspired by this human capability, we introduce the Active Visual Reasoning (AVR) task, extending visual reasoning to partially observable, interactive environments. AVR necessitates agents to: (1) actively acquire information via sequential physical actions, (2) integrate observations across multiple steps for coherent reasoning, and (3) dynamically adjust decisions based on evolving visual feedback. To rigorously evaluate AVR, we introduce CLEVR-AVR, a simulation benchmark featuring multi-round interactive environments designed to assess both reasoning correctness and information-gathering efficiency. We present AVR-152k, a large-scale dataset that offers rich Chain-of-Thought (CoT) annotations detailing iterative reasoning for uncertainty identification, action-conditioned information gain prediction, and information-maximizing action selection, crucial for training agents in a higher-order Markov Decision Process. Building on this, we develop PhysVLM-AVR, an MLLM achieving state-of-the-art performance on CLEVR-AVR, embodied reasoning (OpenEQA, RoboVQA), and passive visual reasoning (GeoMath, Geometry30K). Our analysis also reveals that current embodied MLLMs, despite detecting information incompleteness, struggle to actively acquire and integrate new information through interaction, highlighting a fundamental gap in active reasoning capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systemss (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21111","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:00Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21107","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21107","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21107","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunuo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baiting","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Baiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayan","familyName":"Mukhopadhyay","name":"Mukhopadhyay, Ayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabor","familyName":"Karsai","name":"Karsai, Gabor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Dubey","name":"Dubey, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ESCORT: Efficient Stein-variational and Sliced Consistency-Optimized Temporal Belief Representation for POMDPs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:51:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:19:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In Partially Observable Markov Decision Processes (POMDPs), maintaining and updating belief distributions over possible underlying states provides a principled way to summarize action-observation history for effective decision-making under uncertainty. As environments grow more realistic, belief distributions develop complexity that standard mathematical models cannot accurately capture, creating a fundamental challenge in maintaining representational accuracy. Despite advances in deep learning and probabilistic modeling, existing POMDP belief approximation methods fail to accurately represent complex uncertainty structures such as high-dimensional, multi-modal belief distributions, resulting in estimation errors that lead to suboptimal agent behaviors. To address this challenge, we present ESCORT (Efficient Stein-variational and sliced Consistency-Optimized Representation for Temporal beliefs), a particle-based framework for capturing complex, multi-modal distributions in high-dimensional belief spaces. ESCORT extends SVGD with two key innovations: correlation-aware projections that model dependencies between state dimensions, and temporal consistency constraints that stabilize updates while preserving correlation structures. This approach retains SVGD's attractive-repulsive particle dynamics while enabling accurate modeling of intricate correlation patterns. Unlike particle filters prone to degeneracy or parametric methods with fixed representational capacity, ESCORT dynamically adapts to belief landscape complexity without resampling or restrictive distributional assumptions. We demonstrate ESCORT's effectiveness through extensive evaluations on both POMDP domains and synthetic multi-modal distributions of varying dimensionality, where it consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods in terms of belief approximation accuracy and downstream decision quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceeding of the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS'25). Code would be available at https://github.com/scope-lab-vu/ESCORT"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21107","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:54Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbo","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jinbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tenghui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tenghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xumin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanjia","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yuanjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hudan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Hudan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zishao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengli","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Shengli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong In","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dong In","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedAlign: A Synergistic Framework of Multimodal Preference Optimization and Federated Meta-Cognitive Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:11:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:17:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, large models have shown significant potential for smart healthcare. However, the deployment of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) for clinical services is currently hindered by three critical challenges: a tendency to hallucinate answers not grounded in visual evidence, the inefficiency of fixed-depth reasoning, and the difficulty of multi-institutional collaboration. To address these challenges, in this paper, we develop MedAlign, a novel framework to ensure visually accurate LVLM responses for Medical Visual Question Answering (Med-VQA). Specifically, we first propose a multimodal Direct Preference Optimization (mDPO) objective to explicitly align preference learning with visual context. We then design a Retrieval-Aware Mixture-of-Experts (RA-MoE) architecture that utilizes image and text similarity to route queries to a specialized and context-augmented LVLM (i.e., an expert), thereby mitigating hallucinations in LVLMs. To achieve adaptive reasoning and facilitate multi-institutional collaboration, we propose a federated governance mechanism, where the selected expert, fine-tuned on clinical datasets based on mDPO, locally performs iterative Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning via the local meta-cognitive uncertainty estimator. Extensive experiments on three representative Med-VQA datasets demonstrate that MedAlign achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming strong retrieval-augmented baselines by up to $11.85\\%$ in F1-score, and simultaneously reducing the average reasoning length by $51.60\\%$ compared with fixed-depth CoT approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21083","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21083","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21083","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssef","familyName":"Megahed","name":"Megahed, Youssef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atallah","familyName":"Madi","name":"Madi, Atallah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dina El","familyName":"Demellawy","name":"Demellawy, Dina El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian D. C.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Adrian D. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Knowledge-Driven Vision-Language Model for Plexus Detection in Hirschsprung's Disease"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T01:42:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:16:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hirschsprung's disease is defined as the congenital absence of ganglion cells in some segment(s) of the colon. The muscle cannot make coordinated movements to propel stool in that section, most commonly leading to obstruction. The diagnosis and treatment for this disease require a clear identification of different region(s) of the myenteric plexus, where ganglion cells should be present, on the microscopic view of the tissue slide. While deep learning approaches, such as Convolutional Neural Networks, have performed very well in this task, they are often treated as black boxes, with minimal understanding gained from them, and may not conform to how a physician makes decisions. In this study, we propose a novel framework that integrates expert-derived textual concepts into a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training-based vision-language model to guide plexus classification. Using prompts derived from expert sources (e.g., medical textbooks and papers) generated by large language models and reviewed by our team before being encoded with QuiltNet, our approach aligns clinically relevant semantic cues with visual features. Experimental results show that the proposed model demonstrated superior discriminative capability across different classification metrics as it outperformed CNN-based models, including VGG-19, ResNet-18, and ResNet-50; achieving an accuracy of 83.9%, a precision of 86.6%, and a specificity of 87.6%. These findings highlight the potential of multi-modal learning in histopathology and underscore the value of incorporating expert knowledge for more clinically relevant model outputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted into the ICAAI 2025 - The 9th International Conference on Advances in Artificial Intelligence"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21083","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Pranav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jimmy","familyName":"Chiun","name":"Chiun, Jimmy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ZING-3D: Zero-shot Incremental 3D Scene Graphs via Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T00:52:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:14:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding and reasoning about complex 3D environments requires structured scene representations that capture not only objects but also their semantic and spatial relationships. While recent works on 3D scene graph generation have leveraged pretrained VLMs without task-specific fine-tuning, they are largely confined to single-view settings, fail to support incremental updates as new observations arrive and lack explicit geometric grounding in 3D space, all of which are essential for embodied scenarios. In this paper, we propose, ZING-3D, a framework that leverages the vast knowledge of pretrained foundation models to enable open-vocabulary recognition and generate a rich semantic representation of the scene in a zero-shot manner while also enabling incremental updates and geometric grounding in 3D space, making it suitable for downstream robotics applications. Our approach leverages VLM reasoning to generate a rich 2D scene graph, which is grounded in 3D using depth information. Nodes represent open-vocabulary objects with features, 3D locations, and semantic context, while edges capture spatial and semantic relations with inter-object distances. Our experiments on scenes from the Replica and HM3D dataset show that ZING-3D is effective at capturing spatial and relational knowledge without the need of task-specific training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:00Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20976","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20976","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20976","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Jiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haokai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Haokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minggao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Minggao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xenophon","familyName":"Evangelopoulos","name":"Evangelopoulos, Xenophon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew I.","familyName":"Cooper","name":"Cooper, Andrew I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"L^2M^3OF: A Large Language Multimodal Model for Metal-Organic Frameworks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:12:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:06:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have demonstrated remarkable reasoning capabilities across diverse natural language tasks. However, comparable breakthroughs in scientific discovery are more limited, because understanding complex physical phenomena demands multifaceted representations far beyond language alone. A compelling example is the design of functional materials such as MOFs-critical for a range of impactful applications like carbon capture and hydrogen storage. Navigating their vast and intricate design space in language-based representations interpretable by LLMs is challenging due to the numerous possible three-dimensional atomic arrangements and strict reticular rules of coordination geometry and topology. Despite promising early results in LLM-assisted discovery for simpler materials systems, MOF design remains heavily reliant on tacit human expertise rarely codified in textual information alone. To overcome this barrier, we introduce L2M3OF, the first multimodal LLM for MOFs. L2M3OF integrates crystal representation learning with language understanding to process structural, textual, and knowledge modalities jointly. L2M3OF employs a pre-trained crystal encoder with a lightweight projection layer to compress structural information into a token space, enabling efficient alignment with language instructions. To facilitate training and evaluation, we curate a structure-property-knowledge database of crystalline materials and benchmark L2M3OF against state-of-the-art closed-source LLMs such as GPT-5, Gemini-2.5-Pro and DeepSeek-R1. Experiments show that L2M3OF outperforms leading text-based closed-source LLMs in property prediction and knowledge generation tasks, despite using far fewer parameters. These results highlight the importance of multimodal approaches for porous material understanding and establish L2M3OF as a foundation for next-generation AI systems in materials discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20976","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:52Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sraavya","familyName":"Sambara","name":"Sambara, Sraavya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sung Eun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sung Eun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoman","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Luyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreya","familyName":"Johri","name":"Johri, Shreya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Baharoon","name":"Baharoon, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Du Hyun","familyName":"Ro","name":"Ro, Du Hyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav","familyName":"Rajpurkar","name":"Rajpurkar, Pranav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"3DReasonKnee: Advancing Grounded Reasoning in Medical Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:54:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:06:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) struggle to ground anatomical regions in 3D medical images and reason about them in a step-by-step manner, a key requirement of real-world diagnostic assessment. This ability is essential for aligning model outputs with the diagnostic workflows clinicians use in practice, enabling trustworthy clinician-AI collaboration. Existing 3D datasets provide localization labels, but none support this \"grounded reasoning\" ability. To address this gap, we introduce 3DReasonKnee, the first 3D grounded reasoning dataset for medical images, which provides 494k high-quality quintuples derived from 7,970 3D knee MRI volumes. Each quintuple includes: (1) the 3D MRI volume, (2) a diagnostic question targeting a specific anatomical region (3) a 3D bounding box localizing the relevant anatomical structures, (4) clinician-generated diagnostic reasoning steps that explicitly detail the 3D reasoning process, and (5) structured severity assessments for the relevant anatomical region. The creation and validation of 3DReasonKnee, involving over 450 hours of expert clinician time for manually segmenting MRIs and generating reasoning chains, ensures its superior quality and clinical relevance. We establish ReasonKnee-Bench to evaluate localization and diagnostic accuracy, providing insight into VLM ability to perform grounding and severity assessment across anatomical regions and diagnostic inquiries. We benchmark five state-of-the-art VLMs, providing baseline performance for ReasonKnee-Bench. By providing this unique resource of expert-annotated 3D reasoning pathways, 3DReasonKnee serves as a repository of orthopedic surgeons' diagnostic expertise and offers a vital testbed for advancing multimodal medical AI systems towards 3D, clinically aligned, localized decision-making capabilities. The dataset can be found in: https://huggingface.co/datasets/rajpurkarlab/3DReasonKnee"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Haworth","name":"Haworth, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juo-Tung","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Juo-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nigel","familyName":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Nigel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji Woong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Ji Woong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masoud","familyName":"Moghani","name":"Moghani, Masoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chelsea","familyName":"Finn","name":"Finn, Chelsea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel","familyName":"Krieger","name":"Krieger, Axel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SutureBot: A Precision Framework \u0026amp; Benchmark For Autonomous End-to-End Suturing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:50:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:06:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic suturing is a prototypical long-horizon dexterous manipulation task, requiring coordinated needle grasping, precise tissue penetration, and secure knot tying. Despite numerous efforts toward end-to-end autonomy, a fully autonomous suturing pipeline has yet to be demonstrated on physical hardware. We introduce SutureBot: an autonomous suturing benchmark on the da Vinci Research Kit (dVRK), spanning needle pickup, tissue insertion, and knot tying. To ensure repeatability, we release a high-fidelity dataset comprising 1,890 suturing demonstrations. Furthermore, we propose a goal-conditioned framework that explicitly optimizes insertion-point precision, improving targeting accuracy by 59\\%-74\\% over a task-only baseline. To establish this task as a benchmark for dexterous imitation learning, we evaluate state-of-the-art vision-language-action (VLA) models, including $π_0$, GR00T N1, OpenVLA-OFT, and multitask ACT, each augmented with a high-level task-prediction policy. Autonomous suturing is a key milestone toward achieving robotic autonomy in surgery. These contributions support reproducible evaluation and development of precision-focused, long-horizon dexterous manipulation policies necessary for end-to-end suturing. Dataset is available at: https://huggingface.co/datasets/jchen396/suturebot"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures, 4 tables, NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:36Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungjun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Sungjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changho","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Changho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suenggwan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Suenggwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinya","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xinya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shourjo Aditya","familyName":"Chaudhuri","name":"Chaudhuri, Shourjo Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederic","familyName":"Sala","name":"Sala, Frederic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Models for Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:28:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:05:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forecasting in the real world requires integrating structured time-series data with unstructured textual information, but existing methods are architecturally limited by fixed input/output horizons and are unable to model or quantify uncertainty. We address this challenge by introducing LLM-integrated Bayesian State space models (LBS), a novel probabilistic framework for multimodal temporal forecasting. At a high level, LBS consists of two components: (1) a state space model (SSM) backbone that captures the temporal dynamics of latent states from which both numerical and textual observations are generated and (2) a pretrained large language model (LLM) that is adapted to encode textual inputs for posterior state estimation and decode textual forecasts consistent with the latent trajectory. This design enables flexible lookback and forecast windows, principled uncertainty quantification, and improved temporal generalization thanks to the well-suited inductive bias of SSMs toward modeling dynamical systems. Experiments on the TextTimeCorpus benchmark demonstrate that LBS improves the previous state-of-the-art by 13.20% while providing human-readable summaries of each forecast. Our work is the first to unify LLMs and SSMs for joint numerical and textual prediction, offering a novel foundation for multimodal temporal reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:18Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20951","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20951","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20951","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mattie","familyName":"Tesfaldet","name":"Tesfaldet, Mattie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam W.","familyName":"Harley","name":"Harley, Adam W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos G.","familyName":"Derpanis","name":"Derpanis, Konstantinos G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek","familyName":"Nowrouzezahrai","name":"Nowrouzezahrai, Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Pal","name":"Pal, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generative Point Tracking with Flow Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:25:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:05:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tracking a point through a video can be a challenging task due to uncertainty arising from visual obfuscations, such as appearance changes and occlusions. Although current state-of-the-art discriminative models excel in regressing long-term point trajectory estimates -- even through occlusions -- they are limited to regressing to a mean (or mode) in the presence of uncertainty, and fail to capture multi-modality. To overcome this limitation, we introduce Generative Point Tracker (GenPT), a generative framework for modelling multi-modal trajectories. GenPT is trained with a novel flow matching formulation that combines the iterative refinement of discriminative trackers, a window-dependent prior for cross-window consistency, and a variance schedule tuned specifically for point coordinates. We show how our model's generative capabilities can be leveraged to improve point trajectory estimates by utilizing a best-first search strategy on generated samples during inference, guided by the model's own confidence of its predictions. Empirically, we evaluate GenPT against the current state of the art on the standard PointOdyssey, Dynamic Replica, and TAP-Vid benchmarks. Further, we introduce a TAP-Vid variant with additional occlusions to assess occluded point tracking performance and highlight our model's ability to capture multi-modality. GenPT is capable of capturing the multi-modality in point trajectories, which translates to state-of-the-art tracking accuracy on occluded points, while maintaining competitive tracking accuracy on visible points compared to extant discriminative point trackers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://mtesfaldet.net/genpt_projpage/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20951","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20884","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20884","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20884","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranamya","familyName":"Kulkarni","name":"Kulkarni, Pranamya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puranjay","familyName":"Datta","name":"Datta, Puranjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Burak","familyName":"Varıcı","name":"Varıcı, Burak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emre","familyName":"Acartürk","name":"Acartürk, Emre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthikeyan","familyName":"Shanmugam","name":"Shanmugam, Karthikeyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Tajer","name":"Tajer, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ROPES: Robotic Pose Estimation via Score-Based Causal Representation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T17:42:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Causal representation learning (CRL) has emerged as a powerful unsupervised framework that (i) disentangles the latent generative factors underlying high-dimensional data, and (ii) learns the cause-and-effect interactions among the disentangled variables. Despite extensive recent advances in identifiability and some practical progress, a substantial gap remains between theory and real-world practice. This paper takes a step toward closing that gap by bringing CRL to robotics, a domain that has motivated CRL. Specifically, this paper addresses the well-defined robot pose estimation -- the recovery of position and orientation from raw images -- by introducing Robotic Pose Estimation via Score-Based CRL (ROPES). Being an unsupervised framework, ROPES embodies the essence of interventional CRL by identifying those generative factors that are actuated: images are generated by intrinsic and extrinsic latent factors (e.g., joint angles, arm/limb geometry, lighting, background, and camera configuration) and the objective is to disentangle and recover the controllable latent variables, i.e., those that can be directly manipulated (intervened upon) through actuation. Interventional CRL theory shows that variables that undergo variations via interventions can be identified. In robotics, such interventions arise naturally by commanding actuators of various joints and recording images under varied controls. Empirical evaluations in semi-synthetic manipulator experiments demonstrate that ROPES successfully disentangles latent generative factors with high fidelity with respect to the ground truth. Crucially, this is achieved by leveraging only distributional changes, without using any labeled data. The paper also includes a comparison with a baseline based on a recently proposed semi-supervised framework. This paper concludes by positioning robot pose estimation as a near-practical testbed for CRL."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"A preliminary version of this paper appeared at NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Embodied World Models for Decision Making"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20884","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:41Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20877","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20877","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20877","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoquan","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Baoquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghua","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multimodal Negative Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T11:47:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal learning systems often encounter challenges related to modality imbalance, where a dominant modality may overshadow others, thereby hindering the learning of weak modalities. Conventional approaches often force weak modalities to align with dominant ones in \"Learning to be (the same)\" (Positive Learning), which risks suppressing the unique information inherent in the weak modalities. To address this challenge, we offer a new learning paradigm: \"Learning Not to be\" (Negative Learning). Instead of enhancing weak modalities' target-class predictions, the dominant modalities dynamically guide the weak modality to suppress non-target classes. This stabilizes the decision space and preserves modality-specific information, allowing weak modalities to preserve unique information without being over-aligned. We proceed to reveal multimodal learning from a robustness perspective and theoretically derive the Multimodal Negative Learning (MNL) framework, which introduces a dynamic guidance mechanism tailored for negative learning. Our method provably tightens the robustness lower bound of multimodal learning by increasing the Unimodal Confidence Margin (UCoM) and reduces the empirical error of weak modalities, particularly under noisy and imbalanced scenarios. Extensive experiments across multiple benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness and generalizability of our approach against competing methods. The code will be available at https://github.com/BaoquanGong/Multimodal-Negative-Learning.git."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20877","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:30Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Roger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashanth Gurunath","familyName":"Shivakumar","name":"Shivakumar, Prashanth Gurunath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Bulyko","name":"Bulyko, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankur","familyName":"Gandhe","name":"Gandhe, Ankur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yile","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incentivizing Consistent, Effective and Scalable Reasoning Capability in Audio LLMs via Reasoning Process Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T06:18:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The role of reasoning in Audio Large Language Models remains widely underexplored, as introducing a reasoning process often degrades rather than improves performance during inference, a phenomenon we term test-time inverse scaling, where longer reasoning chains yield progressively worse results. We demonstrate that this stems not from fundamental limitations of reasoning itself, but from inadequate training: models without proper guidance for the reasoning process produce hallucinatory, inconsistent reasoning that accumulates errors over longer chains. To address these challenges, we introduce CESAR (Consistent, Effective, and Scalable Audio Reasoners), shifting from outcome verification to rewarding the reasoning process. Our online reinforcement learning framework employs Group Relative Policy Optimization with a multi-faceted reward suite that incentivizes not only correctness and format but also consistency, structured analytical patterns, causal reasoning, domain-knowledge integration, and calibrated reasoning depth. CESAR resolves test-time inverse scaling, transforming reasoning from detriments into gains while revealing model-specific ``reasoning sweet spots\", where performance peaks during test-time scaling. We achieve state-of-the-art results on MMAU Test-mini, substantially outperforming Gemini 2.5 Pro and GPT-4o Audio, and near-human-level performance on MMSU reasoning tasks. Through AI-as-judge evaluations and qualitative comparisons, we provide both quantitative and qualitative validation of our improved reasoning quality. Importantly, enhanced reasoning creates synergistic effects, simultaneously improving multimodal reasoning and perception capabilities. Overall, CESAR establishes a principled method for developing robust and scalable reasoning in Audio LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"49 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20859","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20859","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20859","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Craig","familyName":"Sanders","name":"Sanders, Craig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Billy","familyName":"Dickson","name":"Dickson, Billy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahaj Singh","familyName":"Maini","name":"Maini, Sahaj Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Nosofsky","name":"Nosofsky, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zoran","familyName":"Tiganj","name":"Tiganj, Zoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vision-language models learn the geometry of human perceptual space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T16:58:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In cognitive science and AI, a longstanding question is whether machines learn representations that align with those of the human mind. While current models show promise, it remains an open question whether this alignment is superficial or reflects a deeper correspondence in the underlying dimensions of representation. Here we introduce a methodology to probe the internal geometry of vision-language models (VLMs) by having them generate pairwise similarity judgments for a complex set of natural objects. Using multidimensional scaling, we recover low-dimensional psychological spaces and find that their axes show a strong correspondence with the principal axes of human perceptual space. Critically, when this AI-derived representational geometry is used as the input to a classic exemplar model of categorization, it predicts human classification behavior more accurately than a space constructed from human judgments themselves. This suggests that VLMs can capture an idealized or `denoised' form of human perceptual structure. Our work provides a scalable method to overcome a measurement bottleneck in cognitive science and demonstrates that foundation models can learn a representational geometry that is functionally relevant for modeling key aspects of human cognition, such as categorization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20859","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20856","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20856","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20856","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FPT-Noise: Dynamic Scene-Aware Counterattack for Test-Time Adversarial Defense in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T08:29:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs), such as CLIP, have demonstrated remarkable zero-shot generalizability across diverse downstream tasks. However, recent studies have revealed that VLMs, including CLIP, are highly vulnerable to adversarial attacks, particularly on their visual modality. Traditional methods for improving adversarial robustness, such as adversarial training, involve extensive retraining and can be computationally expensive. In this paper, we propose a new Test-Time defense: Feature Perception Threshold Counterattack Noise (FPT-Noise), which enhances the adversarial robustness of CLIP without costly fine-tuning. Our core contributions are threefold: First, we introduce a Dynamic Feature Modulator that dynamically generate an image-specific and attack-adaptive noise intensity parameter. Second, We reanalyzed the image features of CLIP. When images are exposed to different levels of noise, clean images and adversarial images exhibit distinct rates of feature change. We established a feature perception threshold to distinguish clean images from attacked ones. Finally, we integrate a Scene-Aware Regulation guided by a stability threshold and leverage Test-Time Transformation Ensembling (TTE) to further mitigate the impact of residual noise and enhance robustness.Extensive experimentation has demonstrated that FPT-Noise significantly outperforms existing Test-Time defense methods, boosting average robust accuracy from 0.07% to 56.86% under AutoAttack while maintaining high performance on clean images (-1.1%). The code will be made public following the publication of the study. The code will be made public following the publication of the study."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11pages,4figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20856","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:01Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20835","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20835","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20835","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dezhi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Dezhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingying","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Qingying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hokin","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Hokin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking the Simulation vs. Rendering Dichotomy: No Free Lunch in Spatial World Modelling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-15T03:06:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spatial world models, representations that support flexible reasoning about spatial relations, are central to developing computational models that could operate in the physical world, but their precise mechanistic underpinnings are nuanced by the borrowing of underspecified or misguided accounts of human cognition. This paper revisits the simulation versus rendering dichotomy and draws on evidence from aphantasia to argue that fine-grained perceptual content is critical for model-based spatial reasoning. Drawing on recent research into the neural basis of visual awareness, we propose that spatial simulation and perceptual experience depend on shared representational geometries captured by higher-order indices of perceptual relations. We argue that recent developments in embodied AI support this claim, where rich perceptual details improve performance on physics-based world engagements. To this end, we call for the development of architectures capable of maintaining structured perceptual representations as a step toward spatial world modelling in AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Space in Vision, Language, and Embodied AI"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20835","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:40:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:40:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:40:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":56,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-26T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-27T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28multimodal+OR+%22multi-modal%22+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision-language-action%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28multimodal+OR+%22multi-modal%22+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision-language-action%22%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100"}}